# Commit Style

Use this format when committing changes and when titling pull requests (e.g. via `agent-commit-push`, the `create-pr` / `worktree-pr` skills, or manual commits).
It follows [Conventional Commits](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/ja/v1.0.0/), the same as myfans-web's `.claude/commands/commit.md`.
If the repository has its own commit command or rule (`.claude/commands/commit.md`, `.agents/commands/commit.md`, commitlint), that wins.

## Message Format

```
<type>(<scope>): <subject>

<body>

<footer>
```

- **No emoji** anywhere in the subject or the PR title. Do not copy emoji from the existing `git log`; older commits may still have them.
- **type** (required): one of the types below.
- **scope** (optional): add it when the affected area is clear (e.g. `fix(payment): ...`, `feat(creator-dashboard): ...`). Without a scope, drop the parentheses too: `<type>: <subject>`, never `feat(): ...`. The same applies to the `${scope}` templates below and in the skills.
- **subject** (required): what changed, briefly, in the repository's language (Japanese for getozinc repositories). End with the imperative form (「〜する」「〜を追加」「〜を修正」) and no trailing period (句点).
- **body** (optional): why the change was made. What changed is readable from the diff, so keep it minimal.
- **footer** (optional): references (`Refs: #1234`, `Closes: #5678`) or a breaking change (`BREAKING CHANGE: ...`).

## Types

| type     | usage                                              |
| -------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| feat     | new feature                                        |
| fix      | bug fix                                            |
| refactor | code cleanup that does not change behavior         |
| perf     | performance improvement                            |
| docs     | documentation only                                 |
| test     | add or fix tests                                   |
| build    | build or dependency changes (package.json, lockfiles) |
| ci       | CI configuration                                   |
| chore    | anything else                                      |
| style    | formatting or whitespace that does not change meaning |
| revert   | revert an earlier commit                           |

## Commit Order

`chore → build → ci → docs → style → refactor → perf → feat → fix → test`

## Scope Guidelines

- `db` — schema, migrations, D1/Drizzle
- `api` or `server` — API routes, server logic
- `web` or `app` — pages, components, client UI
- `infra` — docker, .github/, wrangler, deployment
- `deps` — package.json, lockfiles
- `openclaw` — OpenClaw/Moltworker agent prompts and config
- When changes span multiple scopes: pick the most impactful scope, or use `core`

## Commands (agent commits only)

Set `GIT_AUTHOR_*` / `GIT_COMMITTER_*` to your agent identity before commit:

```bash
git add -A -- <file1> <file2> <fileN>
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME="..." GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL="..." GIT_COMMITTER_NAME="..." GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL="..." git commit -m "${type}(${scope}): ${subject}" -m "${body}"
```

## Staging Rules

- Follow `.gitignore` strictly. Never stage `.env`, `.cursor/**` (except commands), credentials
- Group by intent/responsibility, not only by folder
